---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Preventing Basement Flooding During Maryland Storms | Tips';
const description = 'Practical ways Ellicott City and Westminster homeowners can reduce the risk of basement flooding during heavy Maryland rains and storms.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="py-12">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <nav class="text-sm text-neutral-500 mb-6" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2">/</span>
        <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-primary">Blog</a>
        <span class="mx-2">/</span>
        <span>Preventing Basement Flooding</span>
      </nav>
      
      <header class="mb-8">
        <time class="text-sm text-neutral-500" datetime="2025-08-03">August 3, 2025</time>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mt-2 mb-4">Preventing Basement Flooding During Maryland Storms</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Central Maryland receives its share of heavy rain. A few proactive steps can significantly reduce the chance that stormwater ends up in your basement.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose prose-neutral max-w-none space-y-6 text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Keep Gutters and Downspouts Clear</h2>
        <p>Clogged gutters force water to overflow near the foundation. Clean gutters regularly and ensure downspouts discharge at least several feet away from the house. Extensions or splash blocks help move water farther from the foundation.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Check Grading Around the Foundation</h2>
        <p>Soil should slope away from the house so surface water does not pool against foundation walls. Over time, settling or landscaping changes can reverse this slope. Addressing low spots reduces hydrostatic pressure during storms.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Maintain Sump Pumps</h2>
        <p>If your home has a sump pump, test it periodically by pouring water into the pit. Consider a battery backup or water-powered backup for power outages, which often accompany severe storms. Keep the discharge line clear and directed away from the foundation.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Inspect Window Wells and Exterior Openings</h2>
        <p>Window wells can fill with water and overflow into basement windows. Covers and proper drainage help. Also check that exterior doors and any low openings are properly sealed and that weatherstripping is intact.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Know Your Local Flood Risk</h2>
        <p>Some neighborhoods in and around Ellicott City have histories of flash flooding. Understanding whether your property sits in a higher-risk area helps you prioritize prevention and know when to move valuables upstairs during heavy rain forecasts.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">When Prevention Is Not Enough</h2>
        <p>Even well-maintained homes can experience water intrusion during extreme events. If water does enter your basement, prompt extraction and thorough drying limit long-term damage. Mold can begin growing within a day or two in damp conditions, so timely professional help is valuable.</p>

        <p class="bg-neutral-50 p-6 rounded-xl border border-neutral-100 mt-8">
          If your basement has already taken on water, or you want guidance specific to your property, reach out. We serve Ellicott City, Westminster, and nearby communities. Call <a href="tel:+14105550199" class="text-primary font-semibold">(410) 555-0199</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
